+++<p><strong>PM brief — Offline vs low network vs 5 second wait (OQ-1)</strong></p>
+++
+++<p>Three different things often get mixed up. They need different product decisions.</p>
+++
+++<h4>1. True offline (no network)</h4>
+++<p>The device cannot reach the internet — airplane mode, no signal, or Wi‑Fi with no route. We can detect this <strong>immediately on the device</strong>; we do not need to wait for Adobe to fail first.</p>
+++<p>If the user had a successful online session before, content cards can be saved on the device. Showing those saved cards is <strong>fast</strong> (well under a second once the app asks for them).</p>
+++<p><strong>User expectation:</strong> &quot;Show me what I had last time I was online.&quot;</p>
+++<p><strong>Problem today:</strong> Many apps call <em>network update first, then read cards</em>. When offline, the update hangs waiting for Adobe. The read request waits in line behind that failed update. The user sees a blank screen or error for ~5 seconds — even though saved cards were on the device the whole time.</p>
+++
+++<h4>2. Low network (connected but slow)</h4>
+++<p>The phone thinks it is online, but the connection is poor — congested park Wi‑Fi, weak LTE. Adobe may still respond, but it can take <strong>3 to 10+ seconds</strong>.</p>
+++<p>This is <strong>not the same as offline</strong>. Fresh campaigns may still arrive if we wait long enough.</p>
+++<p><strong>Why a 200ms timeout is risky as a default:</strong> On slow Wi‑Fi, Adobe might return a new promotion in 2–3 seconds. If we give up at 200ms and always show saved (older) content, users on degraded networks would <strong>almost always see stale cards</strong>, even when fresh content was about to arrive. That is a product tradeoff — speed vs freshness — not a simple technical fix.</p>
+++<p><strong>Recommended approach:</strong> Show saved cards <strong>immediately</strong>, then refresh in the background when the network responds. User always sees something; UI updates when fresh content arrives.</p>
+++
+++<h4>3. What the 5 seconds actually means (this is not &quot;disk is slow&quot;)</h4>
+++<p>When customers report a ~5 second wait or timeout, it is usually <strong>not</strong> because reading from device storage is slow. Reading saved cards is typically under 200ms.</p>
+++<p>The 5 seconds is the <strong>maximum time the app waits for the SDK to respond</strong> to a &quot;get my cards&quot; request. Often the delay happens because:</p>
+++<ul>
+++<li>The app asked for a <strong>network update</strong> first, and the <strong>read request is waiting in line</strong> behind that update, or</li>
+++<li>The network update is still waiting on Adobe on a slow or offline connection.</li>
+++</ul>
+++<p><strong>Simple analogy:</strong> Saved cards are a ready-made meal in the fridge. A network update is ordering delivery. If the app always orders delivery first and will not serve the fridge meal until delivery fails, the user stays hungry for 5 seconds even though food was already in the fridge.</p>
+++<p><strong>Disk is fast. Waiting in line behind network work is slow.</strong></p>
+++
+++<h4>What we propose</h4>
+++<table class="wrapped"><tbody>
+++<tr><th>Situation</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
+++<tr><td><strong>Offline</strong></td><td>Detect locally → skip unnecessary network call → show saved cards right away</td></tr>
+++<tr><td><strong>Low network</strong></td><td>Show saved cards first → background refresh with a sensible timeout (e.g. 3–5s) → update UI if fresh content arrives</td></tr>
+++<tr><td><strong>Good network</strong></td><td>Normal update flow; 5 second API limit is acceptable</td></tr>
+++</tbody></table>
+++
+++<h4>Configurable timeout (similar to Optimize)</h4>
+++<p>Like Optimize sync settings at app/Launch level, we could let integrators choose how long a <strong>background network refresh</strong> waits before giving up and keeping saved content — e.g. 3 seconds for availability-first apps, longer for freshness-first apps.</p>
+++<p>This is an <strong>app/network policy</strong>, not something authors set per content card in AJO.</p>
+++<p><strong>200ms should be treated as a disk-read performance target, not a universal network timeout.</strong></p>
+++
+++<p><em>Full detail in §6.1 on this page.</em></p>
