+++<h2>6. Callouts &amp; open questions</h2>
+++<p>This section captures product decisions, known behaviors, and unresolved questions. <strong>Not implementation detail.</strong></p>
+++
+++<h3>6.1 Callout — Offline content may take up to 5 seconds to appear</h3>
+++<ac:structured-macro ac:name="warning" ac:schema-version="1" ac:macro-id="callout-5s-001"><ac:rich-text-body>
+++<p><strong>Observed behavior:</strong> When the device is offline and the app chains <code>updatePropositions</code> → <code>getPropositions</code> (or <code>getContentCardsUI</code>), cached content may not appear for <strong>up to 5 seconds</strong> — sometimes longer if the update waits on Edge timeout (~10s) first.</p>
+++<p><strong>Why it happens today:</strong></p>
+++<ul>
+++<li><code>getPropositions</code> events are processed on the same <code>eventsQueue</code> as <code>updatePropositions</code> — a get waits behind an in-flight network update.</li>
+++<li>Public API uses Event Hub <strong>5s callback timeout</strong> waiting for Messaging to respond.</li>
+++<li>Disk hydrate itself is fast (&lt;200ms); the delay is <strong>queue blocking + timeout</strong>, not disk I/O.</li>
+++</ul>
+++<p><strong>Is 5 seconds acceptable?</strong> <em>Open for product.</em> Proposed mitigation: process read APIs immediately (bypass queue for gets) + integrator cache-first pattern (get before update). Target: sub-second offline render when cache exists.</p>
+++</ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro>
+++
+++<h3>6.2 Callout — Memory-first, then persisted storage</h3>
+++<ac:structured-macro ac:name="info" ac:schema-version="1" ac:macro-id="callout-mem-001"><ac:rich-text-body>
+++<p><strong>Proposed read order (all get APIs):</strong></p>
+++<ol>
+++<li><strong>In-memory cache</strong> — session-qualified propositions from current or prior successful update in this session.</li>
+++<li><strong>Disk hydrate</strong> — only when memory is <code>nil</code> or empty for the requested surface; load saved rules, run qualification, populate memory.</li>
+++<li><strong>Return</strong> — qualified propositions or empty array (not error).</li>
+++</ol>
+++<p>No integrator flag. Apps do not choose memory vs disk — SDK decides automatically.</p>
+++</ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro>
+++
+++<h3>6.3 Callout — Inbox requires container-item AND cards</h3>
+++<ac:structured-macro ac:name="warning" ac:schema-version="1" ac:macro-id="callout-inbox-001"><ac:rich-text-body>
+++<p><code>InboxUI.processInboxPropositions</code> needs both the <code>.inbox</code> container-item (layout, capacity, heading) and individual content card propositions. Caching cards alone is insufficient — offline inbox shows error if container-item is missing.</p>
+++<p><strong>Validation approach:</strong> Simulation tested persisting cards but not container-item → cold-start offline inbox errors as expected. Final behavior requires both to persist when <code>offlineAvailable</code> is true.</p>
+++</ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro>
+++
+++<h3>6.4 Callout — CBE offline caching (proposed)</h3>
+++<ac:structured-macro ac:name="info" ac:schema-version="1" ac:macro-id="callout-cbe-001"><ac:rich-text-body>
+++<p>Code-Based Experiences (<code>.jsonContent</code>, <code>.htmlContent</code>, <code>.defaultContent</code>) follow the same architecture as Content Cards: separate disk key (<code>codeBasedPropositions</code>), same <code>offlineAvailable</code> gate, same memory-first read via <code>getPropositionsForSurfaces</code>. Host apps render JSON/HTML from returned proposition <code>data.content</code>.</p>
+++</ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro>
+++
+++<h3>6.5 Callout — Dismissed cards on cold start (Option B)</h3>
+++<ac:structured-macro ac:name="note" ac:schema-version="1" ac:macro-id="callout-dismiss-001"><ac:rich-text-body>
+++<p>Dismiss writes <code>dismiss</code> to Event History; journey rules may later insert <code>disqualify</code>. Until then, a dismissed card may briefly reappear when loaded from disk on cold start. <strong>Option B</strong> (evict from memory + disk on <code>track(.dismiss)</code>) is open — differs from IAM which keeps rules on disk.</p>
+++</ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro>
+++
+++<h3>6.6 Callout — InboxUI refresh coupling</h3>
+++<ac:structured-macro ac:name="note" ac:schema-version="1" ac:macro-id="callout-inbox-refresh-001"><ac:rich-text-body>
+++<p>Today <code>InboxUI.performRefresh</code> chains <code>updatePropositions</code> completion → <code>getPropositions</code>. Network failure does not mean propositions are unavailable if disk has data. Once disk hydration ships, this pattern works if get runs after failed update and hydrates. Long-term: consider decoupling refresh (get first, update in background) — same cache-first pattern as Content Cards.</p>
+++</ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro>
+++
