+++<h3>Detailed pros and cons</h3>
+++<ac:structured-macro ac:name="info" ac:schema-version="1" ac:macro-id="inbox-offline-context-001"><ac:rich-text-body><p><strong>Inbox context:</strong> For <code>getInboxUI</code>, offline requires <strong>both</strong> the <code>.inbox</code> container-item (layout, capacity, heading) <strong>and</strong> all linked content card propositions on the same inbox surface (<code>i1</code>). Cards alone are insufficient — partial persistence shows error UI on cold start offline. Authors may forget whether a surface was configured for offline unless the flag is visible at channel/surface level.</p></ac:rich-text-body></ac:structured-macro>
+++
+++<h4>Option 1 — Channel configuration (per surface / channel path)</h4>
+++<p><strong>What it is:</strong> One checkbox on the Content Cards (or Inbox) channel. All cards — and for Inbox, the container-item plus linked cards — delivered on that surface inherit the same offline policy.</p>
+++<p><strong>Pros</strong></p>
+++<ul>
+++<li>Simple for marketers — set once per feed/surface, not per card.</li>
+++<li>Predictable behavior — every card on <code>home/feed</code> behaves the same offline.</li>
+++<li>Aligns with surface model — SDK keys persistence by surface; channel ≈ surface path.</li>
+++<li>Natural fit for Inbox — one toggle on inbox surface <code>i1</code> covers container-item and all linked cards together.</li>
+++<li>Easier QA — test offline per surface, not per campaign variant.</li>
+++<li>Lower authoring friction — no per-message decision on every card.</li>
+++<li>Visible surface-level state — authors can see &quot;this surface is offline-enabled&quot; in channel config, reducing &quot;did I cache this?&quot; confusion.</li>
+++</ul>
+++<p><strong>Cons</strong></p>
+++<ul>
+++<li>Channel reuse is opaque — same channel can back multiple journeys/surfaces; authors may not know every consumer, so one toggle affects unknown placements.</li>
+++<li>Backward compatibility is risky — changing channel config retroactively changes offline behavior for all live and future campaigns on that channel without per-campaign visibility.</li>
+++<li>Coarse control — can&apos;t exclude time-sensitive or personalized cards on the same feed.</li>
+++<li>Cross-team governance — channel owned by platform team; journey authors may not see or control the flag.</li>
+++<li>Migration ambiguity — existing channels: default <code>true</code> vs <code>false</code> when UI ships affects every downstream campaign at once (OQ-3).</li>
+++<li>Inbox still channel-dependent — if authors configure CC channel offline but forget inbox channel <code>i1</code>, offline Inbox errors even when cards exist on disk.</li>
+++</ul>
+++
+++<h4>Option 2 — Content / action editor (per card or message)</h4>
+++<p><strong>What it is:</strong> Checkbox in the content editor when authoring each card or action.</p>
+++<p><strong>Pros</strong></p>
+++<ul>
+++<li>Fine-grained control — authors opt in/out per message (promo yes, live score no).</li>
+++<li>Explicit intent — offline is a deliberate choice at creation time.</li>
+++<li>Safer for mixed feeds — same surface can mix offline-eligible and online-only cards.</li>
+++<li>Clear audit trail — flag travels with the campaign/journey version in payload.</li>
+++<li>Matches journey mental model — &quot;this specific message should survive offline.&quot;</li>
+++</ul>
+++<p><strong>Cons</strong></p>
+++<ul>
+++<li>Legacy content gap — older campaigns/journeys have no flag; behavior depends on SDK default (OQ-3: <code>true</code> = silent cache everything; <code>false</code> = nothing offline until re-authored).</li>
+++<li>SDK version skew — customers on older SDK may ignore the flag; same campaign behaves differently by app version.</li>
+++<li>Re-edit burden — enabling offline for existing content requires opening and republishing each message.</li>
+++<li>Authoring fatigue — checkbox on every card; easy to miss or set inconsistently.</li>
+++<li>Inbox complexity — container-item and cards must both be flagged; partial config → error UI offline (see §5.4).</li>
+++<li>Easy to forget cache state — authors may flag individual cards but forget inbox container or linked surface; no single &quot;this surface is cached&quot; view.</li>
+++<li>Weak fit for Inbox UI — Inbox needs container + cards as one unit; per-card flags don&apos;t map cleanly to surface-level offline behavior.</li>
+++</ul>
+++
+++<h4>Option 3 — Launch / extension configuration (global app policy)</h4>
+++<p><strong>What it is:</strong> Checkboxes in Launch (or extension config), e.g. &quot;Offline availability: Content Cards&quot;, &quot;CBE&quot;, &quot;Inbox&quot;. Applies app-wide for that property — all eligible content in that family caches when enabled.</p>
+++<p><strong>Pros</strong></p>
+++<ul>
+++<li>Conscious opt-in — integrator/platform team decides at deploy time; clear ownership.</li>
+++<li>Single switch for rollout — enable offline for entire app without touching AJO campaigns.</li>
+++<li>Backward compatible for authoring — no change to existing campaigns; policy lives in Launch, not message payload.</li>
+++<li>Consistent across SDK versions — once new SDK ships, Launch config drives behavior uniformly.</li>
+++<li>Works before authoring UI — can ship SDK + Launch toggle before AJO UI is ready.</li>
+++<li>No per-message memory burden — authors don&apos;t need to remember per-card or per-channel flags.</li>
+++</ul>
+++<p><strong>Cons</strong></p>
+++<ul>
+++<li>No per-message control — when enabled, all eligible CC/CBE/Inbox content caches; marketers can&apos;t exclude sensitive or ephemeral content.</li>
+++<li>All-or-nothing per surface family — can&apos;t mix offline CC with online-only CC in the same app.</li>
+++<li>Launch ↔ AJO disconnect — marketers don&apos;t see offline policy in journey UI; &quot;why isn&apos;t this updating offline?&quot; support risk.</li>
+++<li>Authors lose cache visibility — no signal in AJO whether a surface or Inbox is cached; only Launch admins know app-wide policy.</li>
+++<li>Multi-app / multi-surface — one Launch property may map to many surfaces; single checkbox may be too broad or too narrow.</li>
+++<li>Inbox same problem at app level — enabling &quot;Inbox offline&quot; caches all inbox surfaces; disabling it breaks all offline Inbox with no per-surface override.</li>
+++<li>Payload vs config split — if flag is Launch-only, server must inject <code>mobileParameters.offline</code> at runtime, or SDK reads Launch directly — extra pipeline work vs Options 1/2.</li>
+++</ul>
